The Nautilus DevOps team is crafting jobs in the Kubernetes cluster. 
While they're developing actual scripts/commands, they're currently setting up templates and testing jobs with dummy commands. 
Please create a job template as per details given below:

Create a job named countdown-datacenter.

The spec template should be named countdown-datacenter (under metadata), and the container should be named container-countdown-datacenter

Utilize image debian with latest tag (ensure to specify as debian:latest), and set the restart policy to Never.

Execute the command sleep 5

Note: The kubectl utility on the jump-host has been configured to work with the Kubernetes cluster.

SOLUTION

yaml
apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: Job
metadata:
  name: countdown-datacenter
spec:
  template:
    metadata:
      name: countdown-datacenter
    spec:
      restartPolicy: Never
      containers:
      - name: container-countdown-datacenter
        image: debian:latest
        command: ["sleep"]
        args: ["5"]
Now let me apply this Job to the cluster:

bash
cat <<EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: Job
metadata:
  name: countdown-datacenter
spec:
  template:
    metadata:
      name: countdown-datacenter
    spec:
      restartPolicy: Never
      containers:
      - name: container-countdown-datacenter
        image: debian:latest
        command: ["sleep"]
        args: ["5"]
EOF
Explanation of the configuration:

Field	Value	Description
metadata.name	countdown-datacenter	Name of the Job
spec.template.metadata.name	countdown-datacenter	Name of the Pod template
spec.template.spec.restartPolicy	Never	Pod won't be restarted on failure
containers[0].name	container-countdown-datacenter	Container name
containers[0].image	debian:latest	Debian image with latest tag
command / args	sleep 5	Executes sleep 5
Verify the Job was created:

bash
kubectl get jobs
kubectl describe job countdown-datacenter
Check the pod status (it will complete after ~5 seconds):

bash
kubectl get pods -l job-name=countdown-datacenter
You should see the Job created successfully, and the pod will transition from Running to Completed after the 5-second sleep command finishes.


